---
/*
  About, rebuilt 2026-08-02 as a story (DESIGN-PLAN section 22).

  This is the one page where the owner's text appears COMPLETE and UNBROKEN, in
  his order, nothing cut. Everywhere else on the site carries an excerpt of it;
  here it exists whole, so there is always one place a reader can go and get the
  brand in its own words rather than in fragments we chose.

  THE FORM. One photograph with a single line over it, then a descent: a screen
  of text, a photograph, the next screen of text, and so on. The text sets the
  pace and the photographs are the evidence for it. Paragraphs come from the
  owner's own blank lines, so HE controls where the page breathes; we do not
  re-cut his writing to fit a layout.

  Two registers, chosen by length rather than by rule. His short declamatory
  paragraphs take the statement register; his long discursive ones take body.
  Uppercasing a sixty-word sentence at statement size is shouting, and the
  register exists to serve the voice, not the other way round.

  WHOSE WORDS. His, so no draft mark. In Italian they are our translation of
  his, so the page says exactly that, once, at the top. See lib/voice.ts: an
  "unapproved draft" would wrongly imply the brand has not decided what it says.

  Reduced from a full screen: the opening photograph is the only sealed screen,
  and every frame after it is "tall", so the next block always peeks.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import MediaSurface from "../../components/MediaSurface.astro";
import Mosaic from "../../components/Mosaic.astro";
import {noteBlock, productBlock, weaveMosaic, zip, type MosaicBlock} from "../../lib/mosaic";
import PairedSurface from "../../components/PairedSurface.astro";
import {getSiteSettings, isPlaceholderText} from "../../lib/content";
import {LOCALES, localePath, pick, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {isMarked, voiceState} from "../../lib/voice";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";
import {SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS} from "../../lib/marks";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({params: {lang}}));
}

const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const settings = await getSiteSettings();

const about = pick(settings.about, locale);
const aboutIsPlaceholder = isPlaceholderText(about);
const openingLine = pick(settings.aboutOpeningLine, locale);
const openingMedia = settings.aboutOpeningMedia;
const photographs = settings.aboutMedia ?? [];

/*
  aboutIsDraft means "we wrote this", which is now false: the story is his. What
  remains is whether this language is one he approved.
*/
const voice = voiceState(settings, locale, Boolean(settings.aboutIsDraft));
const marked = SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS && isMarked(voice) && Boolean(about) && !aboutIsPlaceholder;
const noticeKey = voice === "translation" ? "translationNotice" : "draftNotice";

/*
  His blank lines are the paragraph breaks. Short paragraphs get the statement
  register, long ones get body; the threshold is a word count, not a guess about
  which sentence is more important.
*/
const paragraphs = (about ?? "")
  .split(/\n\s*\n/)
  .map((p) => p.trim())
  .filter(Boolean);
const isShort = (p: string) => p.split(/\s+/).length <= 18;

/*
  THE PEOPLE. His own text names them (2026-08-04), so the page ends on a
  partnership: the two creators, the shop they work with, and the portrait.
*/
const portrait = settings.designerPortrait;
const creators = settings.creators ?? [];
const partnerName = settings.partnerName?.trim() || null;
const partnerUrl = settings.partnerUrl?.trim() || null;

/*
  NO CUT-OUTS ON THIS PAGE (2026-08-17, section 123, the OWNER's call).

  It took two of the six until now — both Oblivion, worn open over jeans and
  worn as a dress — and they have gone to /process, where the other four
  already were. So the whole set lives in one place apart from the home two-up,
  and this page is photographs and video only.

  That is a better division than the old one. A cut-out is a garment lifted off
  its ground and laid on pale paper: it is a way of showing the WORK, which is
  what /process is for. This page is about where the brand and its material
  come from, and a figure floating on white says nothing about either.
*/

/*
  AND THREE WRITTEN BLOCKS (2026-08-13, section 118): Naples, Solofra, and the
  alchemy. OURS, flagged `aboutNotes`.

  THEY WERE THE MAKING, AND THE MAKING HAS A PAGE (2026-08-16, section 122).
  Two of the three said what a processNote already said — the cut that follows
  the animal against THE CUT, the piece that is never finished against WHAT
  LASTS — which is why this page read as /process with different photographs.
  They are replaced by the one thing only this page can say: where the brand
  and its material come from.

  SOLOFRA IS WRITTEN AS A FACT ABOUT THE MATERIAL and never as a claim about
  the brand, because it is the only concrete evidence behind the sustainability
  argument the site makes anywhere. A town an hour up the road that has tanned
  hides for centuries is a supply chain a reader can picture; "sustainable" is
  a word a reader can do nothing with. The schema asks for exactly this: never
  an adjective about the brand.

  The order is the story — where it is from, what it is made of, what it is
  named after — so the alchemy closes instead of opening.

  THEY DO NOT TOUCH HIS STORY. His whole text is above, in one column, complete
  and unbroken — which is what section 22 asks of this page and what section 107
  rebuilt it into. These sit BELOW it, inside the mosaic, beside the
  photographs. A reader meets his voice first and ours only in the margins of
  the pictures, which is the correct order for a brand's own about page.
*/
const notesVoice = voiceState(settings, locale, true);
/*
  THE LEATHER SECTION (section 133). Ours, so it carries the same mark every
  sentence we wrote on his behalf carries; empty means the section is simply not
  on the page, which is a choice rather than a fault.
*/
const materialNote = settings.aboutMaterial;
const materialText = materialNote ? pick(materialNote.text, locale) : null;
const material = materialText
  ? {heading: materialNote?.heading ? pick(materialNote.heading, locale) : null, text: materialText}
  : null;
const materialMarked = SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS && isMarked(voiceState(settings, locale, true));

const notes = (settings.aboutNotes ?? [])
  .map((n) => noteBlock(n, (f) => pick(f, locale), SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS && isMarked(notesVoice), t(locale, "draftNotice")))
  .filter((b): b is MosaicBlock => b !== null);

/* Notes alone into the mosaic now: the cut-outs that used to be zipped in
   beside them are on /process (section 123). */
const mosaicBlocks: MosaicBlock[] = weaveMosaic(photographs.slice(1), notes);
---

{/* The chrome floats on the opening frame (section 115), or the band stays if
    there is no opening frame to float on. */}
<Base shopChrome={false}
  locale={locale}
  chromeOver={openingMedia?.overlay ?? null}
  title={t(locale, "about")}
  description={openingLine ?? undefined}
  image={openingMedia ? imageUrl(openingMedia.poster, 1200) : null}
>
  {/*
    THE ARRIVAL: one photograph, one line. The line is a whole sentence of his,
    unaltered, and it is deliberately the shortest one he wrote.
  */}
  {
    openingMedia ? (
      <MediaSurface media={openingMedia} locale={locale} priority>
        <h1 class="type-display text-display">{openingLine ?? t(locale, "about")}</h1>
      </MediaSurface>
    ) : (
      <TextSurface class="flex min-h-svh flex-col justify-center">
        <h1 class="type-display text-display">{t(locale, "about")}</h1>
        <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{"{ABOUT_OPENING_PHOTOGRAPH}"}</p>
      </TextSurface>
    )
  }

  {/*
    The mark, once, at the top of the story rather than beside every paragraph.
    Repeating it down the page would turn a single honest caveat into noise.
  */}
  {
    marked && (
      <TextSurface class="pt-u8">
        <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{t(locale, noticeKey)}</p>
      </TextSurface>
    )
  }

  {!about && (
    <TextSurface class="py-u13">
      <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{"{ABOUT}"}</p>
    </TextSurface>
  )}

  {
    about && aboutIsPlaceholder && (
      <TextSurface class="py-u13">
        <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{about}</p>
      </TextSurface>
    )
  }

  {/*
    THE DESCENT. A paragraph, then a photograph, then the next paragraph. If
    there are more paragraphs than frames, or the other way round, the leftovers
    simply continue: the page degrades to plain prose, or to a sequence of
    photographs, without a rewrite.
  */}
  {/*
    HIS WHOLE STORY IN ONE COLUMN, BESIDE ONE PHOTOGRAPH (2026-08-13, section
    107), which is what both references do and what this page did not.

    It gave each paragraph its own paired surface: four screens to carry a
    hundred and twenty words, one 30-word paragraph floating beside a
    photograph at a time. Rick Owens sets an entire show note — eight or ten
    paragraphs — in a single narrow column with the photography beside and below
    it; Vivienne Westwood's heritage pages stack five or six paragraphs against
    one image. Neither ever gives a paragraph a screen.

    So the passage is one column now, in his order, unbroken, which is also what
    section 22 asks of this page: it is the one place his text appears COMPLETE.
    The frames that were carrying a paragraph each are freed and run below as a
    mosaic, which is where the density the owner is asking for actually comes
    from — more photographs visible at once, not more space between them.
  */}
  {about && !aboutIsPlaceholder && (
    <PairedSurface media={photographs[0] ?? null} locale={locale} side="left">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-u5">
        {paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
          <p
            class:list={[
              isShort(paragraph) ? "type-display text-display" : "type-body text-body",
              "whitespace-pre-line",
              marked && "draft-text",
            ]}
          >
            {paragraph}
          </p>
        ))}
      </div>
    </PairedSurface>
  )}

  {/*
    THE REST OF THE PHOTOGRAPHY, AS AN ACTUAL MOSAIC (2026-08-12, section 115).

    It was two equal columns of viewport-height crops, which section 107 called
    a mosaic and which the 77 frames say is not one. Same correction as
    /process, same component, same reasons — see Mosaic.astro, which carries the
    measurements and the frames they came from.

    WHY IT MATTERS MORE HERE. This page's whole argument since section 107 is
    that his story goes in ONE COLUMN and the freed frames run below it, and the
    density that buys is supposed to come from more photographs visible at once.
    Two equal columns of full-height crops shows two. The mosaic shows three or
    four, at three or four sizes, which is what `RO14` and `RO19` do.
  */}
  <Mosaic blocks={mosaicBlocks} locale={locale} />

  {/*
    THE LEATHER, AS FACTS (2026-08-16, section 133). The owner asked for a
    sustainability section and this is it, written as things that are checkable
    rather than as things that sound good.

    WHY THE SHAPE IS THE ARGUMENT. Since 2026 a seller in Europe cannot call a
    product sustainable or eco-friendly without being able to prove it, and
    vague green wording is precisely what is looked at. Ciro is the one who
    would answer for a sentence here. So every line is either a fact about the
    material, a fact about where it is tanned, or a property of the object, and
    each one is already true elsewhere on this site.

    WHAT IS NOT HERE IS THE POINT AS MUCH AS WHAT IS. No adjective about the
    brand, no comparison with anyone else, no number nobody measured, and no
    heading with the word sustainability on it, which would frame the facts
    under it as a claim and invite exactly the reading the rest avoids.

    OURS, flagged `aboutMaterial`, so `npm run launch-check` counts it and he
    can rewrite any of it in the studio.
  */}
  {material && (
    <TextSurface class="hairline-h flex flex-col gap-u5 py-u13">
      {material.heading && <h2 class="type-label text-label">{material.heading}</h2>}
      {materialMarked && (
        <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{t(locale, "draftNotice")}</p>
      )}
      <p
        class:list={[
          "type-body text-body max-w-prose whitespace-pre-line",
          materialMarked && "draft-text",
        ]}
      >
        {material.text}
      </p>
    </TextSurface>
  )}

  {/*
    THE TWO CREATORS (2026-08-04). {DESIGNER_BIOGRAPHY} is answered: his own
    approved text names Ferdinando Palmieri and Ciro Cecco, so the page is
    built around a PARTNERSHIP rather than around one portrait.

    Nothing here is invented, and nothing needed to be. His origin passage is
    the last paragraph of the story above; this block only sets the two names
    out as facts, in the label register, and links Ferdressed, because an
    established shop vouching for a young label is the strongest trust signal
    an international buyer gets, and it is a FACT rather than a claim.

    IT IS "EXPERIMENTAL LINE BY", NOT "IN COLLABORATION WITH" (2026-08-16,
    the OWNER's correction). The two are not the same claim: a collaboration
    reads as two labels meeting for one project, and this line came OUT OF
    Ferdressed. His origin passage above has said "experimental line" all
    along; the credit under it disagreed with him, and now does not.

    THE PORTRAIT IS STILL LAST. The page arrives at them.
  */}
  {(creators.length > 0 || partnerName) && (
    <TextSurface class="hairline-h flex flex-col gap-u5 py-u13">
      <h2 class="type-label text-label">{t(locale, "creators")}</h2>
      <ul class="type-display text-display flex flex-col gap-u1">
        {creators.map((person) => <li>{person}</li>)}
      </ul>
      {partnerName && (
        <p class="type-label text-label">
          {t(locale, "experimentalLineBy")}{" "}
          {partnerUrl ? (
            <a href={partnerUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank" class="hairline-under">
              {partnerName}
            </a>
          ) : (
            partnerName
          )}
        </p>
      )}
    </TextSurface>
  )}

  {portrait && <MediaSurface media={portrait} locale={locale} height="tall" />}
</Base>
